# OUTZAAR — Web Frontend

A production-styled React frontend for OUTZAAR, built as a like-for-like upgrade of the
existing Streamlit prototype (`frontend/`, untouched). This app runs entirely on **mock
data** right now. This document explains what exists, how it's wired, and exactly what
the backend needs to provide to replace the mocks.

---

## 1. Stack

| Layer      | Choice                                   |
|------------|-------------------------------------------|
| Build tool | Vite                                       |
| Language   | TypeScript (strict mode)                   |
| UI         | React 18, React Router 6                   |
| Styling    | Tailwind CSS (utility classes, no CSS-in-JS)|
| Motion     | Framer Motion                              |
| Icons      | lucide-react                               |

No backend calls are wired up yet — see §6.

---

## 2. Run it

```bash
npm install
npm run dev        # http://localhost:5173
npm run typecheck  # tsc --noEmit
npm run build       # type-checks, then builds to dist/
```

`vite.config.ts` proxies `/api/*` → `http://localhost:8000/*`, so once FastAPI is running
locally, fetches to `/api/...` from the frontend hit it with no CORS setup needed.

---

## 3. Directory map

```
src/
├── pages/                  One component per route
│   ├── ProspectingPage.tsx
│   ├── ConversationsPage.tsx
│   └── DashboardPage.tsx
├── components/
│   ├── layout/              App shell: sidebar, mobile nav/header, agent status
│   ├── ui/                  Design-system primitives (Button, Badge, Drawer, Dialog, …)
│   ├── prospecting/         ICP input/understanding, prospect card/drawer, filters
│   ├── conversations/       Conversation list, chat thread, AI panel, booking dialog
│   └── dashboard/           Metric strip, pipeline funnel, activity feed, attention list
├── data/                    Mock data + the "AI understanding" logic (see §5)
├── types/                   All shared TypeScript interfaces (see §5)
├── services/api.ts          The ONLY file that should ever be edited to go live (see §6)
├── hooks/useMediaQuery.ts
└── lib/                     Small helpers: cn(), time formatting
```

Nothing outside `src/services/api.ts` and `src/data/*` needs to change when you connect
a real backend — components only ever call functions from `services/api.ts`.

---

## 4. What's implemented, page by page

### AI Prospecting (`/prospecting`)
- Free-text "describe your ideal customer" input, with an inline submit button and
  four example prompts.
- **"OUTZAAR understands" panel** — shows the parsed ICP (industry, location, company
  size, potential need, likely decision makers, buying signals) with a loading state.
- **Improve ICP** button — asks the backend to rewrite/tighten the free-text description.
- **Find Prospects** — fetches and renders company cards (fit score ring, signals,
  pain point preview, top decision maker), with a skeleton loading state and an empty
  state when nothing matches.
- Client-side filter toolbar (minimum fit score slider, location, sort by fit/name) —
  purely local; does not re-hit the backend.
- Clicking a card opens a **detail drawer**: full company overview, fit-score
  reasoning breakdown, buying signals, pain points, all decision makers, and a
  recommended outreach angle. Includes an "Add to outreach" toggle (local state only).

### Conversations (`/conversations`)
- Three-pane layout: conversation list (search + "All / Needs action / Waiting"
  filter) → chat thread → AI panel. Collapses to list↔chat on mobile/tablet, with the
  AI panel becoming a slide-over drawer below `xl` breakpoint.
- Chat thread renders full message history with day dividers, sender grouping, and
  delivery ticks (sending / delivered / read).
- Composer supports Email/WhatsApp channel switching per conversation, "draft with
  OUTZAAR" (fills the suggested response), and optimistic send (message appears
  immediately with a "sending" tick, then reconciles with the backend's reply).
- **AI panel**: intent badge, confidence bar, plain-language summary, recommended
  action, editable suggested response with **Use / Edit / Book Meeting**, and a
  conversation-signals checklist (positive/neutral/negative).
- **Book Meeting** opens a dialog with four proposed slots (next Tue/Wed, two times
  each); confirming appends a system message to the thread and flips status to
  "Meeting opportunity."
- Five conversations are seeded, one for each state: meeting opportunity, interested/
  needs info, follow-up requested, not interested, no response yet.

### Dashboard (`/dashboard`)
- 7-day / 30-day range toggle.
- Metric strip: prospects found, contacts identified, messages sent, replies,
  meetings — each with a delta and a small sparkline.
- **Outbound pipeline** — horizontal funnel (prospects → decision makers → contacted
  → engaged → meetings), expandable per stage for a plain-language readout of
  conversion between stages.
- **AI activity** feed — timestamped log of what "OUTZAAR" has been doing.
- **Needs attention** — cards for conversations requiring action; clicking one
  navigates to `/conversations?c=<id>` and opens that thread directly.

### Global
- Left sidebar (desktop) / bottom nav + top bar (mobile) with an "OUTZAAR AI is
  online" status block.
- One design system throughout: dark navy background, glass-panel cards, blue→violet
  gradients, consistent spacing/typography scale. No emoji anywhere in the UI copy.
- Every async view (ICP understanding, prospect search, conversation list, AI
  analysis, dashboard) has a purpose-built loading skeleton and, where relevant, an
  empty state — nothing spinners-and-blank-screen.

---

## 5. Data model (`src/types/index.ts`)

All 22 exported interfaces are the contract for the backend. The most important ones:

- **`IcpProfile`** — output of "what OUTZAAR understands": `industry`, `location`,
  `companySize`, `potentialNeed`, `decisionMakers: string[]`, `buyingSignals: string[]`,
  plus `industryKeys` (used only by the mock filter — the real backend doesn't need
  to reproduce this field, it's an implementation detail of `src/data/icp.ts`).
- **`Prospect`** — wraps a `Company`, a `fitScore`, `signals`, `painPoints`,
  `decisionMakers: DecisionMaker[]`, `whyIdentified`, `fitReasoning: FitFactor[]`
  (label/score/note triples shown in the drawer), and `outreachAngle`.
- **`Conversation`** — `contact`, `company`, `industry`, `context`, `status`
  (`meeting | interested | followup | declined | awaiting`), `channel`
  (`email | whatsapp`), `messages: Message[]`, and `analysis: AIAnalysis`.
- **`AIAnalysis`** — `intent`, `confidence` (0–100), `summary`, `recommendedAction`,
  `actionKind` (`meeting | send_info | follow_up | close | wait` — drives whether
  "Book Meeting" is enabled), `suggestedResponse`, `signals: ConversationSignal[]`
  (label + `positive | neutral | negative` tone).
- **`DashboardData`** — `ranges: { '7d' | '30d': { metrics, pipeline } }`,
  `activities: Activity[]`, `attention: AttentionItem[]`.

Mock data implementing these shapes lives in `src/data/{prospects,conversations,
dashboard,icp}.ts` — useful as reference payloads when writing FastAPI response
models (e.g. copy `src/data/conversations.ts` into a Pydantic-shaped fixture).

---

## 6. Connecting the FastAPI backend

Every network call the frontend will ever make goes through **`src/services/api.ts`**.
Each exported function already has the suggested route as a comment and a working
mock branch. To go live:

1. Implement the corresponding FastAPI route.
2. Fill in the `request<T>(...)` branch for that function (the helper is already
   written — it does `fetch(`${API_BASE}${path}`)` and JSON-decodes).
3. Set `VITE_USE_MOCK=false` in a `.env.local` (copy `.env.example`).

No component code changes — they only ever call `getProspects()`, `sendMessage()`, etc.

| Function                                   | Suggested route                              | Notes |
|---------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|-------|
| `analyzeIcp(text)`                          | `POST /icp/analyze`                            | Returns `IcpProfile` |
| `improveIcp(text)`                          | `POST /icp/improve`                            | Returns `{ text, profile }` |
| `getProspects(icp)`                         | `POST /prospects/search`                       | Returns `Prospect[]` |
| `getConversations()`                        | `GET /conversations`                           | Returns `Conversation[]` |
| `sendMessage(conversationId, body, channel)`| `POST /conversations/{id}/messages`            | Returns the created `Message` |
| `analyzeConversation(conversationId)`       | `POST /conversations/{id}/analyze`             | Returns `AIAnalysis` |
| `bookMeeting(conversationId, slot, channel)`| `POST /conversations/{id}/meetings`            | Returns a system `Message` |
| `getDashboard()`                            | `GET /dashboard`                               | Returns `DashboardData` |

`API_BASE` defaults to `/api` (proxied to `localhost:8000` in dev via
`vite.config.ts`); override with `VITE_API_URL` for other environments.

---

## 7. Cloudflare Tunnel & app.outzaar.com

The React frontend can be exposed publicly from the local development
machine using Cloudflare Tunnel, without opening router ports or
exposing the machine's public IP.

Domain configuration

outzaar.com is registered with Hostinger, while DNS is delegated to
Cloudflare.

Current Cloudflare nameservers:

kara.ns.cloudflare.com
phil.ns.cloudflare.com

The public application hostname is:

https://app.outzaar.com

Local architecture

When the frontend is running locally, the request path is:

Browser
   │
   ▼
https://app.outzaar.com
   │
   ▼
Cloudflare DNS
   │
   ▼
Cloudflare Tunnel
   │
   ▼
Local machine
   │
   ▼
http://localhost:5173
   │
   ├── Frontend → React + Vite
   │
   └── /api/* → Vite proxy
                  │
                  ▼
            http://localhost:8000
                  │
                  ▼
              FastAPI

The tunnel therefore exposes the Vite frontend on port 5173. API
requests continue to use the existing Vite development proxy and are
forwarded locally to FastAPI on port 8000.

# One-time Cloudflare setup

Install cloudflared if it is not already installed, then authenticate:

cloudflared tunnel login

Create the named tunnel:

cloudflared tunnel create outzaar-app

Create the DNS route:

cloudflared tunnel route dns outzaar-app app.outzaar.com

This creates the Cloudflare DNS mapping for app.outzaar.com to the
tunnel.

Tunnel configuration

Create:

~/.cloudflared/config.yml

with:

tunnel: YOUR-TUNNEL-UUID
credentials-file: /Users/aiyan/.cloudflared/YOUR-TUNNEL-UUID.json

ingress:
  - hostname: app.outzaar.com
    service: http://localhost:5173

  - service: http_status:404

Replace YOUR-TUNNEL-UUID with the UUID returned by:

cloudflared tunnel create outzaar-app

Validate the configuration:

cloudflared tunnel ingress validate

Start the tunnel:

cloudflared tunnel run outzaar-app

Running OUTZAAR locally

- Start the FastAPI backend on port 8000, then start the React frontend:

npm install
npm run dev

The frontend should be available locally at:

http://localhost:5173

With the Cloudflare tunnel running, the same frontend is publicly
accessible at:

https://app.outzaar.com

Important

The Cloudflare tunnel is currently intended for local development/demo
access. Closing the cloudflared tunnel run outzaar-app process
disconnects the public hostname from the local application.

For a permanent production deployment, the frontend should eventually be
deployed to a production hosting environment rather than relying on a
developer laptop and Vite's development server.


## 8. Explicitly not built yet

Per the brief, this phase is frontend/demo only:

- No authentication
- No PostgreSQL / Celery / Redis
- No real Gmail or WhatsApp Business API integration — the channel switch is UI-only
- No real AI model calls — `analyzeIcp`, `improveIcp`, and `analyzeConversation` are
  simple rule-based mocks in `src/data/icp.ts` / `src/data/conversations.ts`, meant to
  be replaced wholesale by real model calls server-side
- No persistence — refreshing the page resets queued prospects, sent-message state,
  etc. (conversation history itself reloads from the mock data each time)

---

## 9. Notes for whoever builds the backend

- `Prospect.fitReasoning` and `Conversation.analysis.signals` are UI-critical — the
  drawer and AI panel are built around showing *why*, not just a score. Keep these in
  any real model output.
- `AIAnalysis.actionKind` directly gates the "Book Meeting" button (disabled when
  `close`). Keep this enum stable or the frontend needs a small tweak.
- `Conversation.status` drives the colored badges (`green/blue/amber/red/slate` —
  mapped in `src/data/conversations.ts` → `statusMeta`). New statuses need a matching
  entry there.
- The mock `getProspects` filters by an internal `industryKey` your backend doesn't
  need to replicate — it's only there so demo data can be filtered without a real
  classifier. Real responses just return whatever `Prospect[]` array is relevant.